\details{
\strong{Simulating from Scratch (Default Method):}
When \code{model} is a character string (\code{"underdamped"} or \code{"overdamped"}), the function generates a completely new dataset. This requires specifying the movement parameters (\code{par}), the number of animals, and the desired measurement error structure.

\strong{Measurement Error Models:}
When simulating observation error, the function can generate noisy locations using either a bivariate Gaussian distribution (\code{obs_dist = "Gaussian"}):
\deqn{(x,y) \sim N((\mu_x,\mu_y), \Sigma),}
or a heavy-tailed bivariate Student's t-distribution (\code{obs_dist = "t"}):
\deqn{(x,y) \sim t_{\nu}((\mu_x,\mu_y), \Sigma),}
where \eqn{(x,y)} are the observed locations, \eqn{(\mu_x,\mu_y)} are the true (latent) locations, \eqn{\Sigma} is the observation error covariance matrix, and \eqn{\nu} represents the degrees of freedom (\code{df} parameter).
The construction of \eqn{\Sigma} depends on the provided \code{measurementError} arguments (e.g., Argos error ellipses or standard deviations). See \code{\link{fitLangevin}}.

\strong{Simulating from a Fitted Model (fitLangevin Method):}
When \code{model} is a \code{fitLangevin} object, the function behaves as a diagnostic and simulation tool.
The \code{conditional} and \code{jointPrecision} arguments define four possible ways to simulate from the fitted model:
\itemize{
  \item \strong{\code{conditional = TRUE, jointPrecision = TRUE}:} Imputes tracks tied to the \code{data}, drawn from the full joint covariance matrix to account for uncertainty. \code{timeStep} is ignored.
  \item \strong{\code{conditional = TRUE, jointPrecision = FALSE}:} Imputes tracks tied to the \code{data}, drawn from the random effects covariance matrix with the movement parameters fixed at their point estimates. \code{timeStep} is ignored.
  \item \strong{\code{conditional = FALSE, jointPrecision = TRUE}:} Starting at the initial location for each track, generates unconstrained tracks forward in time using parameters drawn from the full joint covariance matrix
  \item \strong{\code{conditional = FALSE, jointPrecision = FALSE}:} Starting at the initial location for each track, generates unconstrained tracks forward in time using parameters drawn from the random effects covariance matrix with the movement parameters fixed at their point estimates.
}

\strong{Barrier Constraints:}
When a \code{barrier} is specified, the model restricts animal movement using a physical penalty method for stochastic differential equations (SDEs).
The continuous signed distance function (SDF) created by \code{\link{prepBarrier}}, denoted as \eqn{d(\boldsymbol{\mu})}, is calculated as positive in the unrestricted areas (allowed space) and negative in the restricted areas.

To prevent the animal from entering the restricted zone (where \eqn{d(\boldsymbol{\mu}) \le 0}), the model applies a harmonic oscillator penalty, treating the boundary like a spring. The potential energy (\eqn{U}) of this spring is a quadratic function of the distance traveled into the restricted zone:
\deqn{U(\boldsymbol{\mu}) = \frac{1}{2} \lambda d(\boldsymbol{\mu})^2}

In Langevin dynamics, the force acting on the animal is the negative spatial gradient of the potential energy (\eqn{ -\nabla U(\boldsymbol{\mu})}). Applying the chain rule to the potential energy function yields the exact force applied during simulation and model fitting:
\deqn{\text{Force} = -\lambda d(\boldsymbol{\mu}) \nabla d(\boldsymbol{\mu})}

Because \eqn{d(\boldsymbol{\mu})} is negative in the restricted zone, the equation produces a positive force that pushes the animal in the direction of the shortest path back to the unrestricted area (\eqn{\nabla d(\boldsymbol{\mu})}).

The \code{lambda} parameter acts as the spring constant (\eqn{\lambda}); higher values create a stiffer, harder boundary. This boundary force is added directly to the spatial gradient of the habitat covariates. In continuous time a barrier that animals never cross corresponds to \eqn{\lambda \to \infty}, but the likelihood is built from discrete-time transitions in which the force is evaluated at the start of each step, and these are only stable for \eqn{\lambda \le \lambda_{max}(\sigma, \gamma, \Delta t)} (above it a small excursion into the restricted zone is amplified at every step). \code{fitLangevin} therefore uses \code{lambda = "tied"} by default: at every step the spring constant is set to this limit, computed from the current \code{sigma} and \code{gamma}, i.e. the hardest wall the discretisation can represent. For the underdamped model, with \eqn{x = \gamma\Delta t}, \eqn{\lambda_{max} = \gamma^2 \min(\kappa_1, \kappa_2) / \sigma^2} where \eqn{\kappa_1 = (1-e^{-x})/(1-e^{-x}-x e^{-x})} (binding for \eqn{x \le 3.72}) and \eqn{\kappa_2 = 2(1+e^{-x})/[(1+e^{-x})(x - 1 + e^{-x}) - (1-e^{-x})^2]}; for the overdamped model \eqn{\lambda_{max} = 2/(\sigma^2\Delta t)}. A fixed numeric \code{lambda} describes a soft wall of known stiffness (e.g. data simulated by \code{\link{simLangevin}}), and \code{lambda = "estimate"} treats it as a free parameter, which is only identifiable if observed locations actually enter the restricted zone. For tied fits \code{\link{getUD}} and related functions use \eqn{\lambda = \infty} (zero utilization of the restricted zone). Because \eqn{\lambda_{max}} grows as \eqn{\Delta t} shrinks, the tied wall is only as hard as the latent time grid allows; if fitted tracks touch the barrier, adding latent time points between observations (e.g. 5--10 per interval via \code{predTimes} in \code{\link{formatData}}) makes the wall nearly hard and avoids compensating bias in \code{sigma}, \code{gamma} and \code{beta}.

With \eqn{\lambda = 1/\lambda_D} this quadratic potential is the penalty \eqn{(\boldsymbol{\mu} - \pi(\boldsymbol{\mu}))/\lambda_D} of Delporte, Ditlevsen & Samson (2025, arXiv:2511.22430), where \eqn{\pi} is the projection onto the allowed domain, provided \eqn{d} is the exact Euclidean signed distance. \code{\link{prepBarrier}} computes it exactly from polygons (e.g. a vector coastline), or approximately (to within about half a cell) from a raster mask.

\strong{Barrier onset:} the curvature of \eqn{\frac{1}{2}\min(d,0)^2} jumps from 0 to \eqn{\lambda} at the boundary, which can make the Laplace approximation of the random effects (the inner Hessian) badly behaved when many smoothed locations lie on or near the boundary. \code{barrierOnset} \eqn{= \ell > 0} replaces the potential on the restricted side by
\deqn{B(d) = \ell^2 (t^7/7 - t^6/2 + t^5/2), \quad t = -d/\ell \in [0, 1], \qquad B(d) = \tfrac{1}{2} d^2 + \tfrac{1}{2}\ell d + \ell^2/7, \quad -d > \ell,}
so that \eqn{B} and its first four derivatives vanish at the boundary, and the force \eqn{\lambda B'} rises smoothly over the first \eqn{\ell} of the restricted zone and is thereafter the quadratic spring shifted inwards by \eqn{\ell/2}. The allowed side is unchanged, and \eqn{\ell = 0} recovers the quadratic potential exactly. \eqn{\ell} is in the units of the coordinates (\code{"res"} = one cell of the barrier raster) and is a modelling choice that depends on the species and the data: for animals that do not use the restricted side (e.g. cetaceans and a coastline) a value of the order of the accuracy of the boundary (or one raster cell) is a numerical regularisation of an approximately located boundary; for animals that legitimately use the restricted side (e.g. pinnipeds hauling out on land) a larger value, or a soft, estimated \code{lambda}, is more appropriate. The value is stored with the fit and used by all downstream functions (e.g. \code{\link{getUD}}, \code{\link{simLangevin}}, \code{\link{hierLangevin}}, which requires it to be the same for all individuals).

\strong{Barrier strength (\code{lambda = "tied"}):}
By default \code{simLangevin} uses the same rule as \code{\link{fitLangevin}}: at each simulation step the barrier spring constant is set to the stability limit \eqn{\lambda_{max}(\sigma, \gamma, \Delta t)} of the discretised SDE (see the barrier section below), i.e. the hardest wall the numerical solver can represent at that step. Because \eqn{\lambda_{max}} grows as \eqn{\Delta t} shrinks, the simulated wall is only as hard as \code{timeStep} allows; to mimic animals that never cross a barrier, simulate at a fine \code{timeStep} and use \code{subSample} to obtain the desired observation interval.

For tied simulations the returned object has \code{attr(, "lambda") = "tied"} (unchanged by \code{subSample}, because a hard wall remains a hard wall however often it is observed), so \code{fitLangevin(lambda = NULL)} also fits a tied barrier. For a numeric \code{lambda} the stored value is the numeric stiffness, rescaled to the observation interval when \code{subSample} is used.
}
\examples{
# underdamped model with measurement error

par <- list(beta = c(-4, 6, 5, -0.1),
            sigma = 5,
            gamma = 0.5)

# error ellipse
# exampleCovs included in package; see ?exampleCovs for details
simDat_ee <- simLangevin(par = par,
                      spatialCovs = exampleCovs,
                      measurementError = list(smaj.sd = 1.5,
                                              smin.sd = 0.75,
                                              eor.lim = c(0,180)))
# x- and y-axis errors
simDat_xy <- simLangevin(par = par,
                      spatialCovs = exampleCovs,
                      measurementError = list(x.sd = 1.5,
                                              y.sd = 1.5))

# location quality classes based on provided probabilities
emf_df <- getEMF()

simDat_lc <- simLangevin(par = par,
                         spatialCovs = exampleCovs,
                         measurementError = emf_df)

\dontrun{
# simulate from fitted model
fit <- fitLangevin(data = exampleDat,
                   spatialCovs = exampleCovs)

# unconditional
simFit <- simLangevin(fit,
                      data = exampleDat,
                      spatialCovs = exampleCovs,
                      timeStep = "10 secs")

# conditional on observed tracks
simFit_cond <- simLangevin(fit,
                           data = exampleDat,
                           spatialCovs = exampleCovs,
                           timeStep = "10 secs",
                           conditional = TRUE)

# simulating with a barrier
# create a dummy barrier mask (left half restricted = 0, right half allowed = 1)
coast_barrier <- exampleCovs[[1]]
terra::values(coast_barrier) <- ifelse(terra::crds(coast_barrier)[, "x"]
                                 >= mean(terra::crds(coast_barrier)[, "x"]), 1, 0)
names(coast_barrier) <- "coast_barrier"

# convert mask to SDF and add to the spatial covariates list
exampleCovs_barrier <- exampleCovs
exampleCovs_barrier$coast_barrier <- prepBarrier(coast_barrier)
exampleCovs_barrier$d2coast <- exampleCovs_barrier$coast_barrier / 100

# add a beta coefficient for d2coast to the parameter list
par_barrier <- par
par_barrier$beta <- c(par_barrier$beta, -0.2)

# simulate the data
set.seed(1,kind="Mersenne-Twister",normal.kind="Inversion")
simDat_barrier <- simLangevin(par = par_barrier,
                              nbAnimals = 3,
                              spatialCovs = exampleCovs_barrier,
                              barrier = "coast_barrier",
                              measurementError = list(smaj.sd = 1.5,
                                                      smin.sd = 0.75,
                                                      eor.lim = c(0,180)))

plot(simDat_barrier,beta=par_barrier$beta,
     spatialCovs=exampleCovs_barrier,
     maskRast = coast_barrier)
}
}
